export const PH1={title:'The event and what public health is',hook:'Clean water, vaccines, seat belts, smoke-free laws and food safety inspections save millions of lives, yet most people never notice them. That is public health: protecting and improving the health of whole communities. The HOSA Public Health event asks your team to take a public health issue into your community—and this year’s issue is one that spreads as fast as any virus: health misinformation.',objectives:['Describe the Public Health event, its rounds, display time and scoring.','Define public health and contrast it with clinical medicine.','Describe the 10 Essential Public Health Services.','Describe the roles of the U.S. Public Health Service and the American Public Health Association.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Public Health is a **team event for 2–6 members**, open to middle school, secondary and postsecondary/collegiate divisions. The team creates and delivers a **live community presentation** (up to **10 minutes**) on the annual topic to a real public audience. **Round One** is a pre-judged **video “trailer”** of up to **three minutes** that convinces judges they want to see the full presentation; one member uploads a **public link** by **May 15**. It is worth **90 points**.',
'At ILC, all teams must attend the **Required Project Display Time**, showing their trailer on a battery-powered device (no electricity is provided); **missing it costs 15 points**. The top teams advance to **Round Two**, a **10-minute** in-person presentation—the same one given to the community—worth **115 points**. Teams get **three minutes to set up** and **three to tear down**, must **state the date and audience** of their community presentation first and may use **any presentation tools**.',
'The 2026–27 topic is **“Fact-Check Your Feed: Taking Down Health Myths Before They Spread.”** Teams educate the public to recognize health misinformation, evaluate sources and seek information they can trust.',
{t:'table',caption:'How the event is scored',head:['Part','What is judged','Points'],rows:[['Round One trailer','Length, quality, topic, effectiveness, impact, creativity, “wanting more”','90'],['Display Time','Required attendance at ILC','−15 if missed'],['Round Two content','Community confirmed, importance, understanding, effectiveness, captivating, distinction, research','70'],['Round Two organization','Flow and transitions, opening, closing','15'],['Round Two materials','Visual aids, props, costumes','10'],['Round Two delivery','Voice, stage presence, diction, team participation','20']]}]],
['Defining public health',[
'A classic definition from C.-E. A. Winslow (1920) describes public health as the **science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life and promoting health** through the **organized efforts** of society. Public health focuses on **populations** rather than individual patients and emphasizes **prevention** over treatment. A clinician treats one person with measles; public health works to stop measles from spreading through a community.',
'Public health draws on many fields: **epidemiology**, **biostatistics**, **environmental health**, **health policy**, **health education and communication**, **behavioral science** and more. Its achievements include vaccination, safer workplaces, motor-vehicle safety, safer food, fluoridated water, tobacco control and family planning—improvements that helped add decades to life expectancy in the twentieth century.',
'Health communication is a core public health tool. Accurate, trusted information helps people make healthy choices; misinformation undermines them. That is why this year’s topic is a public health issue, not just a media issue.',
sort('Public health or clinical medicine?',['Public health','Clinical medicine'],[['A city campaign to increase flu vaccination',0],['A doctor prescribing antibiotics for one patient',1],['Testing community water for lead',0],['A nurse dressing a patient’s wound',1],['Teaching residents to spot health myths',0],['A surgeon repairing a broken hip',1]])]],
['The 10 Essential Public Health Services',[
'The **10 Essential Public Health Services** describe what public health systems do, organized around **assessment**, **policy development** and **assurance**, with **equity** at the center: **(1)** assess and monitor population health; **(2)** investigate, diagnose and address health hazards; **(3)** **communicate effectively to inform and educate** people about health; **(4)** strengthen community partnerships; **(5)** create and champion policies and plans; **(6)** use legal and regulatory actions; **(7)** assure an effective system that enables equitable access to services; **(8)** build a diverse and skilled workforce; **(9)** improve and innovate through evaluation, research and quality improvement; and **(10)** build and maintain a strong organizational infrastructure.',
'Your community presentation is a direct example of **Essential Service 3**—communicating to inform and educate—and, if you partner with schools or community groups, **Service 4**. Naming these connections shows judges a deep understanding of public health.',
'The framework was updated in 2020 to emphasize equity: everyone should have a fair opportunity to be healthy, and public health must address the conditions that create unfair differences.',
match('Essential Service and example',[['Assess and monitor population health','Tracking vaccination rates'],['Investigate hazards','Responding to a foodborne outbreak'],['Communicate to inform and educate','A community talk on spotting health myths'],['Strengthen partnerships','Working with a library to host workshops'],['Create policies','Smoke-free park policy'],['Build the workforce','Training future public health professionals']])]],
['The U.S. Public Health Service and APHA',[
'The **U.S. Public Health Service (USPHS)**, the event’s sponsor, traces its roots to 1798 and includes the **USPHS Commissioned Corps**, a uniformed service of more than 6,000 public health professionals—physicians, nurses, pharmacists, engineers, scientists, dentists and others—who serve in agencies such as CDC, FDA, NIH and the Indian Health Service and deploy to public health emergencies. The Corps is led by the **Surgeon General**.',
'The **Surgeon General**, often called “the nation’s doctor,” communicates the best available science to the public through advisories and reports—including the 2021 advisory **Confronting Health Misinformation**. The **American Public Health Association (APHA)** is a professional organization for public health workers that publishes research, advocates for policies and hosts an annual meeting.',
'Both organizations offer resources, career information and examples of how public health professionals communicate with communities.',
flip('Public health organizations',[['USPHS Commissioned Corps','Uniformed public health professionals'],['Surgeon General','Leads the Corps; “the nation’s doctor”'],['2021 advisory','Confronting Health Misinformation'],['APHA','Professional association for public health'],['Event sponsor','U.S. Public Health Service'],['Where officers serve','CDC, FDA, NIH, Indian Health Service and more']])]],
['Why this year’s topic matters',[
'**Health misinformation** is false, misleading or inaccurate health information shared among the public. WHO calls the overwhelming flood of information—including false or misleading information—during health emergencies an **infodemic**, and the **American Psychological Association** has described misinformation as one of the most urgent threats to public health.',
'Misinformation can lead people to **delay needed care**, choose **unproven or harmful treatments**, **refuse effective prevention** such as vaccines and lose **trust in science and health professionals**. Young people spend hours online each day and often share health content with friends and family, so teens can be powerful **fact-checkers** for their communities.',
'Your team’s goal is to help your audience **identify** misinformation, **evaluate** sources and **seek trustworthy information**—and to inspire them to stop myths before they spread.',
chk('Which best defines health misinformation?',['False, misleading or inaccurate health information shared among the public','Any health information you disagree with','Only information from social media','Information from doctors'],'This is the definition in the event guidelines.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Getting started',{a:{text:'Your team of five has chosen Public Health. (Scenario invented for practice.) What must your presentation be?',choices:[['A presentation on the annual topic delivered to a live community audience, up to 10 minutes','b','Correct.',true],['A video only, shown to judges','b','The full presentation must be given in the community.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the Round One trailer for?',choices:[['Convincing judges in three minutes that they want to see the full presentation','c','Correct.',true],['Replacing the community presentation','c','It previews it.',false]]},c:{text:'You plan the community presentation first, then film a trailer that “wows” the judges.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Team of 2–6; a live community presentation (≤10 minutes) on the annual topic; Round One is a 3-minute trailer with a public link uploaded by May 15 (90 points); required ILC display time (−15 if missed); Round Two is the same 10-minute presentation (115 points) after stating the date and audience.','Public health is the science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life and promoting health through organized efforts, focusing on populations and prevention; the 10 Essential Public Health Services include communicating to inform and educate.','USPHS (sponsor) includes the Commissioned Corps led by the Surgeon General, who issued the 2021 advisory on health misinformation; the 2026–27 topic is “Fact-Check Your Feed: Taking Down Health Myths Before They Spread.”'],terms:[['Public health','The science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life and promoting health through organized community efforts.'],['Health misinformation','False, misleading or inaccurate health information shared among the public.'],['Infodemic','An overwhelming amount of information, including false or misleading information, during a health emergency.']],mcqs:[q('How long may the Round One trailer be?','3 minutes','10 minutes','1 minute','30 minutes','Pre-judged.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','Up to 10 minutes','5 minutes','20 minutes','2 minutes','Same as the community presentation.'),q('What happens if a team misses the Required Display Time?','A 15-point deduction','Nothing','Disqualification of all members','Bonus points','From Round Two.'),q('What must teams state before the Round Two presentation begins?','The date and audience of their community presentation','Their GPA','Their favorite topic','The judges’ names','Verification.'),q('Which Essential Public Health Service does a community talk on health myths illustrate?','Communicate effectively to inform and educate','Use legal and regulatory actions','Build organizational infrastructure','None','Service 3.'),q('Who leads the USPHS Commissioned Corps?','The Surgeon General','The CDC director','The President','The APHA president','The nation’s doctor.'),q('Public health focuses mainly on:','Populations and prevention','Individual surgery','Only hospitals','Only medications','Community health.'),q('What is the 2026–27 Public Health topic?','Fact-Check Your Feed: Taking Down Health Myths Before They Spread','Vaping prevention','Heat safety','Hand hygiene','Health misinformation.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain what public health is, how health misinformation threatens it and how a HOSA team’s community presentation fits the work of public health.',rubric:[['Public health',['prevent','prolong life','promote','organized','populations','prevention']],['Misinformation harms',['false','misleading','infodemic','delay care','unproven','vaccines','trust']],['Essential services',['10 essential','communicate','inform','educate','partnerships','equity']],['Event fit',['community','audience','identify','evaluate','trustworthy','usphs','surgeon general']]],model:'Public health is the science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life and promoting health through the organized efforts of society. Unlike clinical medicine, which treats individual patients, public health focuses on populations and prevention—through vaccination programs, clean water, food safety and health education. Health misinformation—false, misleading or inaccurate health information shared among the public—threatens this work. WHO describes the flood of information, including falsehoods, during health emergencies as an infodemic, and the Surgeon General’s 2021 advisory warned that misinformation can lead people to delay care, try unproven treatments, refuse effective prevention such as vaccines and lose trust in health professionals. A HOSA team’s community presentation fits public health directly because one of the 10 Essential Public Health Services is to communicate effectively to inform and educate people about health. By partnering with a school, library or community group, the team also strengthens community partnerships, another essential service. This year’s topic, “Fact-Check Your Feed,” asks teams to teach their audience to recognize misinformation, evaluate sources for credibility and seek information they can trust, helping stop myths before they spread. In doing so, students practice the communication, research and advocacy skills that public health professionals, like those in the U.S. Public Health Service, use every day.'},sources:[RULES,USPHS,APHA,SG_MIS]};

export const PH2={title:'Health misinformation and the infodemic',hook:'During the COVID-19 pandemic, false claims about cures, masks and vaccines spread online faster than health agencies could respond. Some people swallowed toxic substances; others delayed care until it was too late. The problem did not end with the pandemic. Understanding exactly what misinformation is, and how it harms, is the foundation of your presentation.',objectives:['Distinguish misinformation, disinformation and malinformation.','Explain the WHO concept of an infodemic.','Summarize the Surgeon General’s advisory on health misinformation.','Describe the individual and community harms of health misinformation.'],sections:[
['Key definitions',[
'**Misinformation** is false, misleading or inaccurate information shared **without intent to deceive**—for example, a relative forwarding a post they believe is true. **Disinformation** is false information shared **deliberately** to mislead, often for profit, politics or attention. **Malinformation** is genuine information shared out of context to cause harm, such as a real statistic used misleadingly.',
'Not all errors are equal. Some posts are **completely false** (“This tea cures cancer”); others are **misleading** (a true study described with an exaggerated headline), **outdated** (old guidance presented as current) or **missing context** (a rare side effect described as common). Learning these categories helps your audience see that misinformation is not always obvious.',
'Being wrong is human, and science changes as evidence grows. The goal is not to shame people for sharing errors but to build habits that slow the spread of bad information.',
sort('Misinformation or disinformation?',['Misinformation (no intent to deceive)','Disinformation (intent to deceive)'],[['A grandparent shares a false remedy believing it helps',0],['A company invents fake reviews to sell a “miracle” supplement',1],['A student reposts an outdated guideline by mistake',0],['A coordinated account network spreads fake vaccine stories',1],['A friend shares a misleading headline without reading the article',0]])]],
['The infodemic',[
'WHO defines an **infodemic** as **too much information**, including false or misleading information, in digital and physical environments during a disease outbreak. It causes confusion and risk-taking behaviors that can harm health and leads to **mistrust** in health authorities, undermining the public health response. The term blends “information” and “epidemic” because false information can spread like a disease.',
'WHO promotes **infodemic management**: listening to community concerns, communicating clear evidence-based information, building resilience to misinformation and engaging communities. Like an epidemic, an infodemic can be monitored, slowed and prevented.',
'The event topic’s focus on “taking down health myths **before** they spread” echoes this public health approach: prevention is better than cure, for information as well as disease.',
match('Infodemic management action and example',[['Listen to community concerns','Track common questions people ask'],['Communicate clear information','Plain-language posts from health agencies'],['Build resilience','Teach people to check sources'],['Engage communities','Partner with trusted local messengers'],['Monitor','Watch for rising false claims online'],['Respond quickly','Correct a viral myth with accurate facts']])]],
['The Surgeon General’s advisory',[
'In 2021, the U.S. Surgeon General issued **Confronting Health Misinformation**, an advisory calling misinformation an **urgent threat** to public health. It noted that misinformation spreads quickly on social media, can cause confusion, sow mistrust, harm people’s health and undermine public health efforts.',
'The advisory recommended actions for many groups. **Individuals and families** can learn to identify misinformation, verify accuracy before sharing and talk with friends and family with empathy. **Educators** can teach information literacy. **Health professionals** can proactively engage patients and the public. **Journalists**, **technology platforms**, **researchers** and **governments** each have roles, such as improving content moderation, studying misinformation and investing in trustworthy communication.',
'Your presentation can echo these recommendations: equip your audience with skills to verify information and talk with others respectfully.',
match('Group and recommended action',[['Individuals','Verify accuracy before sharing'],['Educators','Teach information and media literacy'],['Health professionals','Engage the public with accurate information'],['Journalists','Avoid sensational headlines and provide context'],['Technology platforms','Reduce spread of harmful misinformation'],['Researchers','Study how misinformation spreads and how to counter it']])]],
['How misinformation harms health',[
'Misinformation causes **direct harms**—taking dangerous substances, using unproven treatments instead of effective care, or stopping prescribed medications. It causes **indirect harms**—delaying screenings or vaccines, which can lead to outbreaks of preventable diseases such as **measles**. It also erodes **trust** in doctors, scientists and public health agencies, making future emergencies harder to manage.',
'Harms fall unevenly. Communities with less access to care, people who face language barriers and groups that have experienced discrimination in health care may be targeted with misinformation or have fewer ways to verify claims. Addressing misinformation is therefore also an **equity** issue.',
'In your presentation, use real, well-sourced examples of harm—such as measles outbreaks in communities with falling vaccination rates—while avoiding blame or stereotypes.',
sort('Direct or indirect harm?',['Direct harm','Indirect harm'],[['Drinking a toxic “cure” seen online',0],['A drop in vaccination leading to a measles outbreak',1],['Stopping insulin because of a false claim',0],['Losing trust in doctors and skipping checkups',1],['Using an unproven cancer treatment instead of chemotherapy',0]])]],
['Recognizing misinformation in the wild',[
'Common **red flags** include: claims of **miracle cures** or “one weird trick”; **emotional** or alarming language (“They don’t want you to know”); **no author or sources**, or sources that do not actually support the claim; **selling a product**; **conspiracy framing**; **anecdotes presented as proof**; **old content** presented as new; and **images or videos** that may be edited, out of context or **AI-generated**.',
'Misinformation increasingly uses **AI-generated** text, images, voices and videos (“deepfakes”) that look professional and may impersonate real doctors. Teach your audience to check whether a real expert actually said something and whether reputable organizations report it.',
'Red flags do not prove a claim is false, but they are signals to **stop and check** before believing or sharing.',
bench('Is the claim supported by the invented post?',[{text:'Invented post: “Doctors HATE this! Drinking lemon water every morning melts belly fat and cures diabetes. Buy our detox powder now—limited time!”',claim:'The post shows several misinformation red flags.',supported:true,why:'Miracle claims, emotional language and a product for sale are classic red flags.'},{text:'Invented post: “Doctors HATE this! Drinking lemon water every morning melts belly fat and cures diabetes. Buy our detox powder now—limited time!”',claim:'The post provides evidence that lemon water cures diabetes.',supported:false,why:'It makes a claim but offers no evidence or sources.'},{text:'Invented post from a county health department: “Flu shots are available free at the library Saturday, 10 a.m.–2 p.m. Bring your insurance card if you have one; no one will be turned away.”',claim:'The post is from an identifiable source and gives specific, checkable details.',supported:true,why:'An identifiable public health source with specific details is a sign of credibility.'},{text:'Invented post: “My cousin used this oil and her cancer disappeared.”',claim:'This anecdote proves the oil cures cancer.',supported:false,why:'One story is not evidence; outcomes need controlled studies.'}])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('A worried parent',{a:{text:'A neighbor shares a viral video claiming a common vaccine is dangerous; she believes it and is worried. (Scenario invented for practice.) Is this misinformation or disinformation from her side?',choices:[['Misinformation—she shares it believing it is true','b','Correct.',true],['Disinformation—she means to deceive','b','She is not trying to deceive.',false]]},b:{text:'How should you respond?',choices:[['With empathy: acknowledge her concern, share trusted sources and suggest she talk with her doctor','c','Correct.',true],['Tell her she is foolish','c','Shaming makes people defensive.',false]]},c:{text:'She agrees to read a pediatrician’s explainer and ask her child’s doctor at the next visit.',end:true}})]],
["A short history of health misinformation",["Health misinformation is not new. In the 1800s, traveling salesmen sold **“snake oil”** and patent medicines with secret ingredients—sometimes alcohol, opium or cocaine—promising to cure nearly anything. Public outrage and investigative journalism helped lead to the **Pure Food and Drug Act of 1906**, an early step toward the modern FDA.","In the twentieth century, tobacco companies spent decades casting doubt on the evidence that smoking causes cancer, a strategy later described as **manufacturing doubt**. Today, the internet and social media allow misinformation to spread to millions in hours, and AI tools can generate convincing fake content cheaply.","What has changed most is **speed and scale**. The core tactics—miracle promises, fake experts, conspiracy claims and doubt about solid science—are old. Teaching audiences to recognize these tactics works across eras and platforms.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Era and example","pairs":[["1800s","Snake oil and patent medicines"],["1906","Pure Food and Drug Act"],["Mid-1900s","Tobacco industry manufacturing doubt"],["1998–2010","Fraudulent MMR study published, then retracted"],["2020s","COVID-19 infodemic on social media"],["Today","AI-generated deepfake “doctors”"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Misinformation is false or misleading information shared without intent to deceive; disinformation is deliberately false; malinformation is real information used out of context to harm; errors can be false, misleading, outdated or missing context.','WHO’s infodemic is too much information, including falsehoods, during outbreaks, causing confusion, risky behavior and mistrust; infodemic management listens, communicates, builds resilience and engages communities.','The 2021 Surgeon General advisory called misinformation an urgent threat and gave roles for individuals, educators, health professionals, journalists, platforms and researchers; harms are direct, indirect and to trust; red flags include miracle cures, emotional language, no sources, selling products and AI fakes.'],terms:[['Disinformation','False information shared deliberately to deceive.'],['Malinformation','Genuine information shared out of context to cause harm.'],['Deepfake','AI-generated audio, image or video that imitates a real person.']],mcqs:[q('Which is disinformation?','A company invents fake reviews to sell a supplement','A grandparent shares a remedy believing it works','A student shares an outdated guideline by mistake','A nurse corrects a myth','Intent to deceive.'),q('WHO describes an infodemic as:','Too much information, including false or misleading information, during an outbreak','A computer virus','A lack of information','A vaccine program','Information + epidemic.'),q('In what year did the Surgeon General issue the health misinformation advisory?','2021','2001','1964','2015','Confronting Health Misinformation.'),q('Which is a red flag for misinformation?','A miracle cure claim with a product for sale','An identifiable health department source','Specific, checkable details','Links to peer-reviewed studies','Stop and check.'),q('A drop in vaccination leading to a measles outbreak is a(n):','Indirect harm','Direct harm','Benefit','Unrelated event','Delayed prevention.'),q('Malinformation is:','Real information shared out of context to cause harm','Always completely false','Accidental sharing','A type of vaccine','Context matters.'),q('Which group should teach information literacy, according to the advisory?','Educators','Only doctors','Only the government','No one','Schools have a role.'),q('A single person’s story about a cure is:','An anecdote, not proof','Strong scientific evidence','A randomized trial','Always false','Needs controlled studies.')],frq:{prompt:'Define health misinformation, disinformation and the infodemic, and explain three ways health misinformation harms individuals and communities.',rubric:[['Definitions',['misinformation','false','misleading','without intent','disinformation','deliberately','malinformation']],['Infodemic',['who','too much information','outbreak','confusion','mistrust']],['Harms',['delay care','unproven','vaccines','measles','toxic','trust','equity']],['Response',['surgeon general','verify','share','empathy','sources','educators']]],model:'Health misinformation is false, misleading or inaccurate health information shared among the public, usually by people who believe it is true and do not intend to deceive. Disinformation is false information spread deliberately to mislead, often for profit or influence, such as fake reviews for a “miracle” supplement, while malinformation is real information taken out of context to cause harm. The World Health Organization uses the term infodemic for the overwhelming amount of information, including false or misleading claims, that spreads during an outbreak, causing confusion and risky behavior and undermining trust in health authorities. Misinformation harms individuals directly when people swallow dangerous substances, stop prescribed medicines or choose unproven treatments instead of effective care. It causes indirect harm when people delay screenings or refuse vaccines, which can lead to outbreaks of preventable diseases like measles that endanger infants and others who cannot be vaccinated. It also damages communities by eroding trust in doctors, scientists and public health agencies, making future emergencies harder to manage, and these harms fall hardest on communities with less access to care or reliable information. The Surgeon General’s 2021 advisory called misinformation an urgent public health threat and urged individuals to verify information before sharing, educators to teach information literacy and health professionals to engage the public with empathy and accurate information.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_INFODEMIC,SG_MIS,APA_MIS]};

export const PH3={title:'Why health misinformation spreads',hook:'A 2018 study of millions of posts found that false news spread farther, faster and deeper than the truth—and people, not bots, were mainly responsible. Misinformation is not spread only by bad actors; it is spread by ordinary people whose brains and feeds are wired in ways that make myths sticky. Knowing why helps your audience resist.',objectives:['Describe cognitive biases that make misinformation believable.','Explain how emotion and novelty drive sharing.','Describe how social media algorithms, echo chambers and bots amplify misinformation.','Identify why certain topics and moments are especially vulnerable.'],sections:[
['How our brains process information',[
'People use mental shortcuts to process huge amounts of information. **Confirmation bias** leads us to favor information that fits what we already believe. The **illusory truth effect** means that repeated claims feel more true, even when they are false—so seeing a myth many times makes it seem familiar and believable. **Motivated reasoning** leads us to accept claims that support our identity or group.',
'Other shortcuts include trusting information that is **easy to process** (simple, confident and visually appealing), relying on **authority cues** (a white coat or “Dr.” in a username) and **anecdotes** that feel more vivid than statistics. None of these makes someone foolish; they are normal features of human thinking that misinformation exploits.',
"Experts, too, can fall for misinformation outside their field, and highly educated people are not immune. That is why systems—like checking sources and asking others—matter more than simply being smart. Presenting this idea respectfully helps audiences admit they might have been fooled without feeling embarrassed.",
'Because repetition strengthens belief, effective corrections avoid repeating the myth more than necessary and lead with the fact.',
match('Bias and description',[['Confirmation bias','Favoring information that fits existing beliefs'],['Illusory truth effect','Repeated claims feel more true'],['Motivated reasoning','Accepting claims that support our identity'],['Authority cue','Trusting a white coat or “Dr.” title'],['Anecdote effect','A vivid story outweighs statistics'],['Processing fluency','Simple, confident claims feel believable']])]],
['Emotion and novelty',[
'Content that triggers strong **emotions**—fear, anger, outrage, surprise or hope—is more likely to be shared. Health topics are naturally emotional because they involve our bodies, our children and our fears of illness. A post saying “This common food is poisoning your kids” spreads faster than a calm summary of nutrition research.',
'**Novelty** matters too. The 2018 study by Vosoughi, Roy and Aral in *Science* found that false news was about **70% more likely to be retweeted** than true news and that false stories were more **novel** and triggered surprise and disgust. People like to share things that seem new and exciting.',
'Teach your audience to notice their own emotional reactions: a strong feeling is a cue to **pause** before sharing.',
sort('Likely to spread quickly?',['Spreads quickly','Spreads slowly'],[['“SHOCKING: doctors hide this cure!”',0],['A calm summary of a nutrition study',1],['A scary video about a “new” disease threat',0],['A detailed health department report',1],['An outraged post blaming a group for an outbreak',0]])]],
['Algorithms, echo chambers and bots',[
'Social media **algorithms** recommend content that keeps people engaged, and emotional, surprising content often gets the most engagement. Over time, feeds can show users more of what they already react to, creating **filter bubbles** and **echo chambers** where people mostly see views like their own and rarely see corrections.',
'**Bots** (automated accounts) and coordinated networks can amplify false claims, making them seem more popular than they are. **Influencers** with large followings can spread misinformation widely, sometimes because they profit from selling products. Even so, research suggests ordinary users sharing content is a major driver of spread.',
'You can teach your audience to **diversify** their feeds by following reputable health sources, to recognize sponsored content and to report harmful misinformation using platform tools.',
flip('Amplifiers of misinformation',[['Algorithm','Recommends engaging content'],['Echo chamber','Mostly seeing views like your own'],['Filter bubble','Personalized feed that hides other content'],['Bot','Automated account that amplifies posts'],['Influencer','Account with a large, trusting audience'],['Sponsored post','Content paid for by a company']])]],
['Vulnerable topics and moments',[
'Misinformation thrives when **uncertainty is high** and good information is scarce—at the start of a new outbreak, after a disaster or when scientific understanding is still developing. These gaps are called **data voids**: when people search for something new, the first results may come from unreliable sources.',
'Some topics attract persistent misinformation: **vaccines**, **cancer treatments**, **nutrition and weight loss**, **supplements and “detoxes,”** **mental health**, **reproductive health** and **sun safety**. Profit motives—selling supplements, courses or devices—drive much of it.',
'Public health communicators try to fill data voids quickly with clear information and to **prebunk** predictable myths before they spread.',
sort('Common misinformation topic?',['Frequent target','Rarely targeted'],[['Vaccine safety',0],['Detox teas and cleanses',0],['Unproven cancer cures',0],['Weight-loss supplements',0],['How to tie shoelaces',1]])]],
['Who shares and why',[
'People share health misinformation for many reasons: **to help** loved ones, to **warn** others, to **belong** to a group, to express **identity** or **values**, because they **distrust** institutions or simply because they **did not check**. Most sharers mean well. Understanding motivations helps you respond with **empathy** rather than ridicule.',
'Trust is central. People are more likely to believe information from **trusted messengers**—family, friends, community leaders, faith leaders and their own clinicians—than from distant institutions. That is why peer-to-peer education, like your community presentation, can be powerful.',
'In your presentation, encourage audience members to become trusted messengers themselves by checking before sharing and gently correcting friends.',
chk('Why do most people share health misinformation?',['They want to help or warn others and did not check','They always intend to deceive','They are paid by bots','They never read anything'],'Most sharers mean well, so respond with empathy.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('A viral claim',{a:{text:'A post claiming a new virus can be cured by gargling salt water gets thousands of shares within hours of the first news reports. (Scenario invented for practice.) Why might it spread so fast?',choices:[['High uncertainty and a data void, plus a simple, hopeful message','b','Correct.',true],['Because it was verified by scientists','b','No evidence was offered.',false]]},b:{text:'You see it in your feed and feel relieved. What should you do?',choices:[['Pause, check reputable sources and avoid sharing until verified','c','Correct.',true],['Share it immediately to help friends','c','Check before sharing.',false]]},c:{text:'You find the health department’s guidance and share that instead.',end:true}})]],
["Building resistance in yourself and others",["Resistance starts with **self-awareness**. Ask yourself: Does this post make me feel strongly? Does it confirm what I already believe? Have I seen it many times without checking? These questions interrupt the shortcuts that misinformation exploits.","Build **better habits**: follow reputable health sources so your feed contains accurate information, turn off autoplay or take breaks from endless scrolling, read beyond headlines and wait before sharing emotional content. Encourage friends to do the same—social norms matter, and when people see others checking sources, they are more likely to check too.","Communities build resistance through **trusted messengers**, **media literacy education** in schools and **prebunking** campaigns before predictable events like flu season or a new vaccine rollout.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Builds resistance?","buckets":["Builds resistance","Increases vulnerability"],"items":[["Reading beyond the headline",0],["Sharing emotional posts immediately",1],["Following reputable health sources",0],["Only seeing views like your own",1],["Asking “Where did this come from?”",0],["Trusting a claim because it is familiar",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Cognitive biases—confirmation bias, the illusory truth effect (repetition makes claims feel true), motivated reasoning, authority cues and anecdotes—make misinformation believable.','Emotional and novel content spreads faster; a 2018 Science study found false news was about 70% more likely to be retweeted; algorithms, echo chambers, bots and influencers amplify it, but ordinary users drive much spread.','Misinformation thrives in uncertainty and data voids and around vaccines, cancer, nutrition, supplements and mental health; most sharers mean well, so respond with empathy and use trusted messengers.'],terms:[['Confirmation bias','The tendency to favor information that fits existing beliefs.'],['Illusory truth effect','Repeated claims feel more true even if false.'],['Echo chamber','An environment where people mostly see views like their own.']],mcqs:[q('Seeing a false claim many times makes it seem more true because of:','The illusory truth effect','Herd immunity','Peer review','Randomization','Repetition.'),q('Favoring information that fits what you already believe is:','Confirmation bias','Recall bias','Selection bias','Lead-time bias','Mental shortcut.'),q('According to the 2018 Science study, false news was about how much more likely to be retweeted?','70%','7%','100 times','Less likely','Vosoughi, Roy and Aral.'),q('Which emotion makes sharing more likely?','Outrage or fear','Boredom','Calm','Indifference','Emotional content spreads.'),q('A gap in reliable information about a new topic is called a:','Data void','Echo chamber','Bot','Filter','Fill it quickly.'),q('Who are trusted messengers?','Family, community leaders and clinicians','Anonymous accounts','Bots','Pop-up ads','Trust drives belief.'),q('Why respond to sharers with empathy?','Most mean well and shaming makes people defensive','Because they are always right','To end the conversation','It is not important','Better outcomes.'),q('Automated accounts that amplify posts are:','Bots','Influencers','Algorithms','Fact-checkers','Coordinated networks.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain three reasons health misinformation spreads quickly and how understanding them can help your community audience resist it.',rubric:[['Psychology',['confirmation bias','illusory truth','repetition','motivated reasoning','anecdote']],['Emotion and novelty',['emotion','fear','outrage','novel','surprise','70%','science']],['Platforms',['algorithm','echo chamber','filter bubble','bots','influencers']],['Resistance',['pause','check','diversify','trusted','empathy','prebunk','data void']]],model:'First, our brains use shortcuts that make misinformation believable. Confirmation bias makes us favor claims that fit what we already believe, and the illusory truth effect means that the more often we see a claim, the more true it feels—even if it is false. Vivid anecdotes and authority cues like a white coat can feel more convincing than statistics. Second, emotional and novel content spreads faster. Health topics trigger fear and hope, and a 2018 study in Science found false news was about 70% more likely to be retweeted than true news, partly because it seemed new and surprising. Third, social media algorithms reward engagement, so emotional posts are recommended more often, while echo chambers mean people may rarely see corrections, and bots and influencers can make claims look more popular than they are. Uncertain moments, like the start of an outbreak, create data voids that misinformation quickly fills. Understanding these reasons helps our audience resist. We can teach them to notice a strong emotional reaction as a cue to pause before sharing, to remember that familiarity is not proof, to diversify their feeds by following reputable health sources and to check claims with trusted organizations. Because most people share misinformation to help others, we encourage them to correct friends with empathy and to become trusted messengers who share accurate information instead.'},sources:[RULES,SCIENCE_2018,APA_MIS,WHO_ONLINE]};

export const PH4={title:'Evaluating health sources: fact-checking your feed',hook:'Professional fact-checkers do something surprising when they land on an unfamiliar health website: they leave it almost immediately and open new tabs to see what others say about the source. This habit—reading laterally—is one of the most effective ways to spot misinformation. This lesson gives your team, and your audience, a practical fact-checking toolkit.',objectives:['Apply the SIFT method and lateral reading.','Evaluate websites, authors and evidence using credibility criteria.','Explain the hierarchy of evidence and peer review.','Recognize sponsored content, AI-generated media and manipulated images.'],sections:[
['The SIFT method',[
'**SIFT** is a quick four-step method developed by digital literacy expert Mike Caulfield. **S—Stop:** pause before you share or react, and ask whether you know the source. **I—Investigate the source:** find out who is behind the information and whether they have expertise or a motive. **F—Find better coverage:** look for trusted sources reporting the same claim. **T—Trace claims, quotes and media to the original context:** find where a statistic, quote or image really came from.',
'SIFT works in **minutes**, which makes it practical for everyday scrolling. It is especially effective for viral health claims, screenshots of “studies” and videos that may be clipped or edited.',
'SIFT is an excellent framework for your community presentation because it is memorable and easy to demonstrate live with an invented example.',
order('SIFT',['Stop','Investigate the source','Find better coverage','Trace claims to the original context'])]],
['Lateral reading',[
'**Lateral reading** means **leaving** a website to check what other sources say about it, rather than reading deeply on the site itself (“vertical reading”). Open new tabs and search the organization’s or author’s name plus words like “funding,” “reliability” or “criticism.” Check whether reputable organizations, such as health agencies or medical schools, cite the source.',
'Websites can look professional and use scientific language while promoting false claims. Lateral reading reveals who funds a site, whether it sells products and whether experts consider it reliable.',
'A useful habit: before reading an unfamiliar health site, spend 60 seconds finding out who runs it.',
sort('Lateral or vertical reading?',['Lateral reading','Vertical reading'],[['Searching the organization’s name plus “funding”',0],['Reading the site’s own “About us” page only',1],['Checking whether a medical school cites the source',0],['Scrolling deeper into the same article for proof',1],['Looking up the author’s credentials elsewhere',0]])]],
['Credibility criteria',[
'MedlinePlus suggests asking: **Who runs the site?** (government .gov, educational .edu and established nonprofit .org sites are often reliable, but check). **Who pays for it?** (advertising and product sales can bias content). **What is the purpose?** (to inform or to sell?). **Where does the information come from?** (cited research and qualified reviewers). **How current is it?** (look for review dates). **Is the claim too good to be true?**',
'Other checklists, such as the **CRAAP test**—**C**urrency, **R**elevance, **A**uthority, **A**ccuracy, **P**urpose—cover similar ideas. For individuals, check **credentials** (a “Dr.” may not be a medical doctor or may be outside their field) and **conflicts of interest**.',
'Domain names are clues, not guarantees: some .org sites promote misinformation, and some commercial sites are accurate. Always combine domain clues with lateral reading.',
flip('CRAAP test',[['Currency','How recent? Has it been updated?'],['Relevance','Does it fit your question?'],['Authority','Who wrote it? Are they qualified?'],['Accuracy','Is it supported by evidence?'],['Purpose','To inform, sell or persuade?'],['Too good to be true?','Probably is']])]],
['Evidence and peer review',[
'Not all evidence is equal. The **hierarchy of evidence** generally places **systematic reviews and meta-analyses** at the top, followed by **randomized controlled trials**, **cohort** and **case-control** studies, **case reports**, and finally **expert opinion** and **anecdotes**. A single small study—or a study in mice or cells—does not prove an effect in people.',
'**Peer review** means experts evaluated a study before publication; it reduces errors but does not guarantee truth. **Preprints** are shared before peer review. Watch for headlines that **exaggerate** results (“cures,” “proves”), confuse **correlation with causation** or ignore the size of an effect.',
'Teach your audience a simple rule: one study is a clue, not a conclusion; look for the **scientific consensus** from major health organizations.',
"Also check the **size** of an effect. A headline claiming a food “doubles your risk” may describe a tiny change, such as from 1 in 10,000 to 2 in 10,000. Asking “double of what?” helps audiences understand absolute risk and avoid unnecessary fear.",
order('Hierarchy of evidence (strongest to weakest)',['Systematic reviews and meta-analyses','Randomized controlled trials','Cohort studies','Case-control studies','Case reports','Expert opinion and anecdotes'])]],
['Sponsored content, AI and manipulated media',[
'Look for labels such as **“sponsored,” “ad,” “paid partnership”** or affiliate links—content paid for by companies may present products favorably. Influencers must disclose paid promotions, but many do not.',
'**AI-generated** text, images and video can impersonate doctors or create fake “news” clips. Signs can include unnatural lip-syncing, odd hands or text in images, robotic voices or accounts with no history. Use **reverse image search** to find where a photo first appeared and check whether the real expert or organization actually made the statement.',
'Screenshots of “studies,” headlines or posts are easy to fake or crop. Trace them to the original source before trusting them.',
sort('Trust more or check carefully?',['More trustworthy signal','Check carefully'],[['A county health department page with a recent review date',0],['A video of a “doctor” with no name or credentials selling pills',1],['A systematic review in a peer-reviewed journal',0],['A cropped screenshot of a headline',1],['A paid partnership post for a supplement',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Fact-checking a post',{a:{text:'A post shows a “doctor” saying sunscreen causes cancer, with a link to an unfamiliar website. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the first SIFT step?',choices:[['Stop—pause before reacting or sharing','b','Correct.',true],['Share it to warn friends','b','Stop first.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you investigate the source?',choices:[['Read laterally: search the website and the “doctor” to see who they are and who funds them','c','Correct.',true],['Read more pages on the same site','c','That is vertical reading.',false]]},c:{text:'You find the site sells “natural” sunscreen alternatives and that dermatology organizations recommend sunscreen. You find better coverage and share it.',end:true}})]],
["Trustworthy health sources to know",["Teach your audience a short list of **reliable starting points**: **MedlinePlus** (from the National Library of Medicine) for plain-language health information; **CDC** and **state and local health departments** for public health guidance; **NIH** institutes for research-based information; **WHO** for global health; and **professional medical organizations**, such as the American Academy of Pediatrics or American Cancer Society, for condition-specific guidance.","Most important, encourage people to ask **their own health care providers**—doctors, nurses, pharmacists—who know their history and can explain evidence in context. Pharmacists are often the most accessible health professionals and can answer questions about supplements and medications.","Even trusted sources can make mistakes or update guidance. Look for the **date** and the **evidence** behind recommendations, and check more than one reliable source for important decisions.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Source and best use","pairs":[["MedlinePlus","Plain-language health information"],["Local health department","Community guidance and services"],["CDC","National public health guidance"],["WHO","Global health information"],["Pharmacist","Questions about medicines and supplements"],["Your doctor or nurse","Advice for your own health"]]}]]
],takeaways:['SIFT: Stop, Investigate the source, Find better coverage, Trace claims to the original context; lateral reading means leaving a site to see what others say about it.','Ask who runs and pays for a site, its purpose, sources and date (MedlinePlus); CRAAP checks currency, relevance, authority, accuracy and purpose; domains and “Dr.” titles are clues, not guarantees.','Hierarchy of evidence: systematic reviews and RCTs above observational studies, case reports and anecdotes; peer review helps but is not proof; one study is a clue, not a conclusion; watch for sponsored content, AI fakes and manipulated screenshots.'],terms:[['SIFT','Stop, Investigate the source, Find better coverage, Trace claims.'],['Lateral reading','Checking other sources about a website instead of reading only the site.'],['Peer review','Expert evaluation of research before publication.']],mcqs:[q('What does the S in SIFT stand for?','Stop','Search','Share','Study','Pause first.'),q('Lateral reading means:','Leaving a site to check what others say about it','Reading every page of a site','Reading sideways','Only reading headlines','Like fact-checkers.'),q('Which question does MedlinePlus suggest asking about a health website?','Who pays for the site?','How many colors does it use?','Is the font large?','Does it have music?','Funding can bias.'),q('Which is at the top of the hierarchy of evidence?','Systematic reviews and meta-analyses','Anecdotes','Expert opinion','Case reports','Combine many studies.'),q('In the CRAAP test, the P stands for:','Purpose','Popularity','Price','Pictures','Inform or sell?'),q('A study in mice shows that:','It may not apply to people','A cure exists for humans','Nothing at all','The results are false','Needs human studies.'),q('Which is a sign of sponsored content?','“Paid partnership” label','A .gov domain','A peer-reviewed citation','A review date','Company-paid.'),q('What does reverse image search help you do?','Find where a photo first appeared','Make photos brighter','Delete images','Create deepfakes','Trace media.')],frq:{prompt:'Teach a community audience how to fact-check a health claim they see online using SIFT, lateral reading and credibility criteria.',rubric:[['SIFT',['stop','investigate','source','find better coverage','trace','original']],['Lateral reading',['leave','new tab','search','funding','reliable','who runs']],['Credibility',['purpose','sell','sources','current','credentials','.gov','too good to be true']],['Evidence',['one study','peer review','systematic review','consensus','correlation','ai','sponsored']]],model:'When you see a health claim online, use SIFT. First, Stop: notice how the claim makes you feel and pause before sharing, especially if it seems shocking or too good to be true. Second, Investigate the source by reading laterally—instead of scrolling deeper into the same site, open a new tab and search the website or author’s name with words like “funding” or “reliability” to learn who runs it, whether they sell products and whether experts trust them. Third, Find better coverage by checking whether trusted sources—such as health departments, medical schools or MedlinePlus—report the same claim. Fourth, Trace claims, quotes and images back to their original context, using reverse image search for photos and finding the actual study behind a headline. As you check, ask the credibility questions: who runs and pays for the site, whether its purpose is to inform or to sell, whether it cites evidence and how current it is. Remember that a “Dr.” title or a professional-looking site is not proof, that sponsored posts and AI-generated videos can mislead and that one study—especially in animals—is a clue rather than a conclusion. Look for the scientific consensus from systematic reviews and major health organizations. If the claim fails these checks, do not share it; share the trustworthy information you found instead.'},sources:[RULES,SIFT,MEDLINE_EVAL,NCCIH]};

export const PH5={title:'Common health myths and how to correct them',hook:'Correcting a myth can backfire if done poorly—repeating it in a headline, lecturing or mocking can make people dig in. Research on debunking shows better ways: lead with the fact, explain why the myth is wrong and show respect. Your presentation will be most effective if it both corrects specific myths and teaches your audience how to correct others.',objectives:['Explain the scientific evidence behind several common health myths.','Use the truth sandwich and other debunking techniques.','Explain prebunking and psychological inoculation.','Talk with friends and family about misinformation respectfully.'],sections:[
['Vaccine myths',[
'The claim that the **MMR vaccine causes autism** began with a small 1998 study that was later found to be fraudulent and was **retracted** in 2010; its lead author lost his medical license. Large studies since then—including a Danish cohort of more than **650,000 children** published in 2019—found **no link** between MMR vaccination and autism. Other myths claim vaccines contain dangerous amounts of ingredients or that “natural immunity” from disease is always better, ignoring the serious risks of the diseases themselves.',
'Falling vaccination rates have led to **measles outbreaks**; measles can cause pneumonia, brain swelling and death and is extremely contagious. When discussing vaccines, share the evidence calmly, acknowledge that it is normal to have questions and encourage people to talk with their health care provider.',
'Avoid making the myth the headline. Instead of “Vaccines do NOT cause autism,” lead with “Large studies of hundreds of thousands of children show the MMR vaccine is safe and does not cause autism.”',
chk('What did the 2019 Danish cohort study of more than 650,000 children find?',['No link between MMR vaccination and autism','MMR causes autism','Vaccines were never studied','Autism is caused by measles'],'Large studies consistently find no link.')]],
['Nutrition, supplements and “detox” myths',[
'Common myths include **detox teas and cleanses** that claim to flush “toxins” (the liver and kidneys already do this), **“superfoods”** that cure disease, **supplements** that melt fat or boost immunity dramatically and **extreme diets** promoted by influencers. Dietary supplements are **not approved by the FDA** for safety and effectiveness before they are sold, and some contain hidden ingredients.',
'Other persistent myths: “**Eating fat makes you fat**” (overall diet quality and calories matter more), “**Carbs are always bad**” (whole grains are part of healthy diets) and “**You need to drink eight glasses of water exactly**” (needs vary). The NIH’s National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health encourages people to **know the science** and talk with their health care providers before using supplements.',
'In your presentation, a myth-versus-fact segment with props—a “detox” tea box, a supplement bottle—can be engaging, as long as you present evidence respectfully.',
sort('Myth or fact?',['Myth','Fact'],[['Detox teas remove toxins your liver cannot',0],['Supplements are not FDA-approved for safety and effectiveness before sale',1],['A superfood can cure cancer',0],['The liver and kidneys filter waste from the body',1],['Whole grains can be part of a healthy diet',1]])]],
['Other common health myths',[
'**Sun safety:** “Sunscreen causes cancer” and “You only need sunscreen on sunny days” are myths; dermatology organizations recommend broad-spectrum sunscreen because UV exposure causes skin cancer, and UV rays reach skin on cloudy days. **Cancer:** “Natural” or alternative treatments used **instead of** proven treatment can be deadly; claims of a hidden cure ignore the many people who work in cancer research.',
'**Mental health:** “Depression is just weakness” and “Talking about suicide puts the idea in someone’s head” are myths that keep people from getting help. **Antibiotics:** they do not work against viruses like colds and flu, and misuse drives resistance. **Cold weather:** being cold does not directly cause colds—viruses do, though people spread them more indoors in winter.',
'Choose myths that matter to **your audience**: younger audiences may encounter skincare, vaping and fitness myths; parents may see vaccine and children’s health myths; older adults may see supplement and medication myths.',
match('Myth and fact',[['Sunscreen causes cancer','UV exposure causes skin cancer; sunscreen protects'],['Antibiotics cure colds','Antibiotics do not work on viruses'],['Cold weather causes colds','Viruses cause colds'],['Talking about suicide plants the idea','Asking directly can open the door to help'],['Natural cures replace chemotherapy','Proven treatment saves lives'],['Depression is weakness','Depression is a treatable condition']])]],
['How to debunk: the truth sandwich',[
'The **Debunking Handbook 2020** recommends a structure often called the **truth sandwich**: **(1) Fact**—lead with the clear, simple truth; **(2) Myth**—warn that a myth is coming and mention it only once; **(3) Fallacy**—explain **why** the myth is wrong and how it misleads (for example, it relies on a retracted study or confuses correlation with causation); **(4) Fact**—end by repeating the truth.',
'Effective corrections are **specific**, come from **trusted messengers**, give an **alternative explanation** that fills the gap the myth leaves, and use **plain language** and visuals. Avoid ridicule; people defend beliefs tied to their identity when they feel attacked.',
'Corrections work best when they are **repeated** and when they come **soon** after people encounter the myth.',
order('Truth sandwich',['State the fact','Warn that a myth is coming and state it once','Explain the fallacy—why the myth is wrong','Repeat the fact'])]],
['Prebunking and psychological inoculation',[
'**Prebunking** warns people about misinformation **before** they see it. Like a vaccine, **psychological inoculation** exposes people to a **weakened** form of a misleading tactic—such as fake experts, cherry-picked data or emotional manipulation—with an explanation of how it works, so they can recognize and resist it later.',
'Research has found that short videos and games teaching manipulation techniques can improve people’s ability to spot misinformation. Prebunking is efficient because it works across many myths: once people recognize a **tactic**, they can spot it in new claims.',
'Your presentation can prebunk by teaching common tactics—**fake experts**, **logical fallacies**, **impossible expectations** (“unless it’s 100% safe, it’s dangerous”), **cherry picking** and **conspiracy theories**—sometimes summarized as **FLICC**.',
flip('FLICC tactics',[['F—Fake experts','Unqualified people presented as authorities'],['L—Logical fallacies','Arguments that do not follow'],['I—Impossible expectations','Demanding 100% certainty'],['C—Cherry picking','Choosing only data that fit'],['C—Conspiracy theories','Claiming a secret plot hides the truth'],['Prebunking','Warning about tactics before people see them']])]],
['Talking with friends and family',[
'Corrections from people we know can be powerful. Start from **shared values** (“I know you want the kids to be healthy—so do I”), ask **open questions** (“Where did you see that?”), **listen** to concerns and share a trusted source rather than arguing point by point. Talk **privately** rather than correcting someone publicly in comments, which can feel embarrassing.',
'Accept that one conversation may not change a mind. Keep the relationship strong, offer to look at evidence together and suggest talking with a trusted clinician. Model good behavior by fact-checking your own posts and correcting yourself when you get something wrong.',
'In your presentation, demonstrate a short, respectful conversation—a skit can make this memorable and gives the audience words to use.',
kase('A family group chat',{a:{text:'Your uncle posts in the family chat that a supplement cures diabetes and you can stop insulin. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is your best first move?',choices:[['Message him privately, ask where he saw it and share your concern for his health','b','Correct.',true],['Mock him in the group chat','b','Public ridicule makes people defensive.',false]]},b:{text:'He says a doctor in a video recommended it. What do you do?',choices:[['Look up the “doctor” together and share guidance from a diabetes organization, suggesting he ask his own doctor before changing medicine','c','Correct. Investigate the source and find better coverage.',true],['Tell him all doctors lie','c','That undermines trust in real care.',false]]},c:{text:'He agrees not to stop his insulin and to ask his doctor at his next visit.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The MMR–autism claim came from a fraudulent 1998 study retracted in 2010; large studies (e.g., 650,000+ Danish children) find no link; falling vaccination leads to measles outbreaks.','Detox, superfood and supplement claims often lack evidence and supplements are not FDA-approved before sale; other myths involve sunscreen, antibiotics, cancer “cures” and mental health—choose myths relevant to your audience.','Debunk with the truth sandwich (fact, myth once, fallacy, fact) from trusted messengers with an alternative explanation; prebunk by teaching tactics (FLICC: fake experts, logical fallacies, impossible expectations, cherry picking, conspiracies); talk privately from shared values with empathy.'],terms:[['Truth sandwich','A correction that starts and ends with the fact and explains the myth’s flaw.'],['Prebunking','Warning people about misinformation tactics before they encounter them.'],['Cherry picking','Selecting only data that support a claim while ignoring the rest.']],mcqs:[q('What happened to the 1998 study linking MMR to autism?','It was retracted in 2010 after being found fraudulent','It won a Nobel Prize','It was confirmed by later studies','It was never published','Discredited.'),q('Which structure is recommended for debunking?','Fact, myth, fallacy, fact','Myth, myth, myth','Fact only','Myth, then nothing','Truth sandwich.'),q('Dietary supplements are:','Not approved by the FDA for safety and effectiveness before sale','Always tested like prescription drugs','Guaranteed safe','Illegal','Know the science.'),q('Do antibiotics work against colds?','No, colds are caused by viruses','Yes, always','Only in winter','Only for children','Misuse drives resistance.'),q('Prebunking works like:','A vaccine for misinformation','A cure after belief','A punishment','A filter bubble','Psychological inoculation.'),q('In FLICC, the I stands for:','Impossible expectations','Information','Influencers','Infodemic','Demanding 100% certainty.'),q('How should you correct a family member’s post?','Privately, from shared values, with a trusted source','By mocking them publicly','By ignoring them forever','By posting a meme about them','Empathy works.'),q('Why lead a correction with the fact, not the myth?','Repetition can make the myth feel more true','Facts are boring','To confuse people','It does not matter','Illusory truth effect.')],frq:{prompt:'Choose one common health myth, explain the evidence against it and write a truth-sandwich correction you could use in your community presentation.',rubric:[['Myth and evidence',['myth','evidence','study','retracted','cohort','no link','fda','organization']],['Truth sandwich',['fact','myth','fallacy','why','repeat']],['Technique',['trusted','plain language','alternative explanation','visual','empathy','not ridicule']],['Audience action',['check','source','doctor','share','prebunk','tactic']]],model:'The myth I would correct is that the MMR vaccine causes autism. The claim began with a small 1998 study of 12 children that was later found to be fraudulent; the journal retracted it in 2010 and the lead author lost his medical license. Since then, large studies have repeatedly found no link—for example, a 2019 Danish study followed more than 650,000 children and found that vaccinated children were no more likely to be diagnosed with autism than unvaccinated children. My truth-sandwich correction would begin with the fact: “The MMR vaccine is safe and protects children from measles, mumps and rubella—and studies of hundreds of thousands of children show it does not cause autism.” Then I would warn that a myth is coming and state it once: “You may have heard a claim that MMR causes autism.” Next comes the fallacy: “That idea came from a tiny study that was found to be fraudulent and retracted. It also confused timing with cause: autism signs often become noticeable around the same age children get MMR, but that does not mean one causes the other.” Finally, I would repeat the fact: “The science is clear—MMR is safe and keeps kids from dangerous diseases like measles.” I would deliver it calmly, acknowledge that it is normal for parents to have questions and encourage them to talk with their child’s health care provider and check trusted sources before sharing.'},sources:[RULES,DEBUNK,HVIID,NCCIH]};
